# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is authored on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true is the common
# default. Without this file a Windows checkout writes CRLF into the working tree,
# and `gofmt -l` - which is both a CI gate and the `lint:go` script - then reports
# every Go file as unformatted, because gofmt normalises to LF. The gate becomes
# unrunnable on the platform the code is written on, so it stops being consulted.
#
# Declaring eol=lf makes the working tree match what the repository stores on every
# platform, so the same command gives the same answer everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF regardless of platform: a CRLF shebang makes Linux
# report "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" and the script cannot run at all.
# Their executable bit is tracked in the index (mode 100755), which Windows does
# not model - use `git update-index --chmod=+x` when adding a new one.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are the inverse: cmd.exe requires CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary content: never subject to line-ending or diff conversion. Content packs in
# particular are checksummed in their manifest, so a single converted byte would
# fail pack validation.
*.db binary
*.db-wal binary
*.db-journal binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.vsix binary

# Generated files: keep them out of diffs and reviews.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff linguist-generated
